A||||14||Ken Wittlief||Digital Camera Panasonic review, photo, $194||||1||
Z||000000||Ken Wittlief||06-25-2001||08:23 AM||||I said I would buy this camera if it got below $200 at BJs and it did, so I did.<P>Panasonic PVDC2590<BR>1280 x 960 resolution<BR>or 640 x 480<P>3X optical zoom <BR>2X digital zoom (takes the center 640 x 480 from the image and only stores that part<P>the optical zoom lens is equal to about 35mm to 110mm zoom lens on a 35mm camera<BR>if you click on the digital zoom too then its like a 75mm to 220mm zoom lens on a 35mm camera - but then you only capture a 640 x 480 image.<P>auto focus range: 8" to infinity<P>built in flash<P>holds two cards, comes with one 8M that will hold: <BR>15 -  1280 x 960 in super fine mode<BR>30 - 1280 x 960 in fine mode<BR>100 - 640 x 480 (normal mode)<P> its also does short movie clip grabs<P>and it comes with a modem card so you can transfer images over the phone line to your computer, or you can have the camera set up remotely, and control it and transfer images with your PC. (neither of which sounds very useful to me).<P>The camera has an optical viewfinder and a color LCD on the back - you can turn the LCD off to save battery power.<P> I think the camera is great, esp for $194!!! I got the last one Bjs had on the shelf, I dont know if they are being discontinued or they ran out of stock.<P>here is a sample photo at the highest res mode. Its 300k jpg compressed - will take a minute to download on a phone line - (I put it on my own server since its so big).<BR>its a photo of my Fiero by the Erie Canal.<P>   <IMG SRC="http://members.aol.com/wittlief/newcam.jpg">   <P>Id say the only weakness of the camera is you have to upload the images to your PC with a serial port connection, which is slow - its takes about an hour to upload the entire 8Meg memory card (all the images) so you essectailly start the upload and go do something else. IT does not use USB.<P>  But since it uses the removable card I am pretty sure I can get a card port for my PC, or an adapter for my notebook, which should let you upload the images by plugging the card into the PC, which will then be almost instantianious.<P>[This message has been edited by Ken Wittlief (edited 06-25-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by Ken Wittlief (edited 06-25-2001).]||||reg||1
