Z||000007||84Bill||06-25-2001||11:31 AM||||LOL on both posts ken. The card reader is the best way to go. It copies my pictures faster than the USB method does. Plus the reader is powered from the keyboard so power is never an issue.||||reg||14
Z||000008||Ken Wittlief||06-25-2001||11:40 AM||||the thing I like the most about the camera is the optical zoom lens, all the way in (out?) is a 2X telephoto - i like the way photos look when taken at 2X<P>its great for portraits, great for cars and boats, great for everything (yes even landscapes)<P> I did some figuring a while back, for a photo, if you take it with a normal lens, or god forbid, a wide angle lens, then when you view the photo you have to hold it a few inches away from your nose to get the right perspective.<P>  with a tele on the camera the proper viewing distance is further back. With the camera set to full tele on the zoom (like the jpg above) then on a 17 or 19 " monitor, the image looks 'right' when viewed up to several feet away.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Ken Wittlief||06-25-2001||01:14 PM||||BTW if you didnt notice already - my rear deck no longer proclaims "Fiero 2M6"<P> brand new razor knife blade took it right off, and little oderless paint thinner took the glue off from under it. <P> There is now a faint ghost of it left in the sivler paint, where the rest of the car has faded, you have to stand in just the right spot to see it.<P> I like it.||||reg||1
Z||000010||SteveJ||06-25-2001||01:18 PM||||Direct exposure to the sun might have been a problem? I never knew that. Camera has over 10,000 photos on it I just figured it had seen it's day.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Ken Wittlief||06-25-2001||02:16 PM||||most cameras have a shutter or lens cap or something that covers the CCD when the camera is off, but if you point it right at the sun when the CCD is 'exposed' then its the same as taking a magnifying glass and holding it over a piece of wood on a sunny day - it will cook the CCD.<P> Since my camera doesnt have a lens cover I am already thinking about a small camera case for it.<P>  CCDs also have a short memory for bright lite. If you pan across a really bright object (spot light or reflection of the sun) and then take a picture a second or so later, the smear of the bright spot will persist into the image.<P>  You use to see this on football games, a white streak off a helmet sometimes as a camera panned or a runner crossed the field of view with the sun reflecting off his helmet. This doent hurt the camera any - pointing it at the sun will.||||reg||1
Z||000012||isthiswhereiputausername?||06-25-2001||03:38 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by btoth:<BR><B>I have the SanDisk USB card reader for my camera.  It's at least as fast as a ZIP drive.  Just drag and drop like any other disk drive... sure beats plugging in the camera and "uploading".</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>I agree completely with the sandisk reader.. Well worth the money.<P>We have a kodak dc215 millemium cam and it works great.<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000013||FieroJoe||06-25-2001||11:44 PM||||Good pics for the money, invest in the USB!  Our digital camera uses smartmedia cars, about the size of a matchbook and hold up to 64 meg on the really expensive ones.  We currently have two 8mb cards, and that is well enough.  Ours is an Olympus Camedia C-2020 Z.  I am not sure how much we paid for it, but our lowest quality pics are 640x480, and the card can take 82 of those I think, and the next level goes up to 800x600.  There is a huge difference in those pics.  I usually take all my pics in 800x600 and then size them down if I'm going to post them or something.  The card will hold 37 of those level pics.<P>The highest resolution that this camera can take is 1600x1200, and it also stores it into TIFF format instead of jpg.  the card can hold 1 of these pics!  I also have never ever used this setting because the need has never presented itself.<P>I love the camera though, I take so many pictures now of my car with every major mod so I can make a "Fiero Scrapbook"<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/joesig_1.jpg"> <BR>'87 Coupe 5spd.<BR>AIM:FieroJoe87||||reg||1
Z||000014||Ken Wittlief||06-26-2001||09:45 AM||||I think most digital photos are gonna end up being viewed primarilly on a monitor - not too many of us have 1600 x 1200 monitors - 1280 x 1024 really requires a 19" monitor or larger, so I figure a 1280 camera is about optimum right now.<P> I printed out some shots on a cannon 720dpi inkjet - looks good on a full page (8 x 10) from a couple feet away. I will have to get the photo quality ink cartridge for the printer and try it, but mostly I will use the images on my PC.<P>  <A HREF="http://www.webshots.com" TARGET=_blank>www.webshots.com</A>  has a free wallpaper and screen saver program that lets you que up a bunch of jpgs for a slide show screen saver - so whenever your pc is not being used, its putting up fullscreen jps every 20 seconds or so - really nice - who need a printer?||||reg||1
